      "side": 1,
      "message": "I think you can use something like the logic below (untested) to avoid discarding any existing buffered data when advancing the counter by a small number. This is worth doing as part of the Poly1305-ChaCha20 AEAD, for example, where we always skip to the end of the first block by setting the counter value to 1 and don\u0027t really want to discard all the values we\u0027ve computed.\n\nI don\u0027t think there is any reason that the counter value needs to be a multiple of the number of blocks buffered, all assembly should be trivially able to handle arbitrary counter values.\n\n    if s.counter \u003c counter + (uint32(s.len)/blockSize) {\n        panic(\"chacha20: Advance attempted to rollback counter\")\n    }\n    if s.counter \u003c counter {\n        // New counter corresponds to a preexisting block of data in the \nbuffer.\n        s.len \u003d int((s.counter - counter) * blockSize)\n    } else {\n        // New counter has advanced beyond any buffered data.\n        s.len \u003d 0\n    }\n    s.counter \u003d counter",
